  "platform": "Mega Drive",
  "summary": "Treasure concentrates its Mega Drive action around formidable bosses and a versatile fighter with weapons, counters and a powerful dash.",
  "body": [
    "Alien Soldier is Treasure’s single-player run-and-gun game, first released for Mega Drive in Japan on 24 February 1995. The player controls Epsilon-Eagle through short stretches of ordinary enemies that quickly lead to much larger opponents. Boss encounters form the centre of the game, with each creature demanding attention to its attacks and vulnerable moments. Six weapon types and several movement techniques give the player a substantial set of tools to learn. The challenge comes from choosing and executing the right response while the next pattern is already unfolding.",
    "Before starting, the player chooses four of the six weapons to carry. Each has its own ammunition reserve, making a change of gun part of managing a fight. One firing style permits movement while shooting; the other fixes Epsilon-Eagle in place for more precise aiming. Double jumping and hovering provide further ways to adjust position around a boss. A weapon’s reach matters together with the room available to stand, jump or stay airborne, so learning an encounter includes learning where each tool can be used safely.",
    "Epsilon-Eagle can counter enemy projectiles and turn them into health when the timing is right. At full health, his dash becomes the damaging Phoenix Force, joining survival and attack in a useful loop: recover, restore the stronger move and choose a moment to pass through the enemy. Hideyuki Suganami led development, with other Treasure staff helping bring the ambitious project to completion. Its late Mega Drive release reached North America through Sega Channel, while Japanese and European players could buy cartridges of this concentrated series of duels."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Epsilon-Eagle",
    "Choosing how to shoot",
    "Turning defence into an opening"
  ],
